Verdict: PASS — the diff does what #1080 asked. I wrote the verdict to session_plan/eval_task_1.md.

• What changed: /bg kill and quitting yoyo now send SIGTERM to the job's process group, wait up to 2 seconds, then SIGKILL whatever is left. When several jobs are running at quit, they share one 2-second window, so quitting takes about 2 seconds at most rather than 2 per job.
• Unchanged: the model's bash tool still sends SIGKILL with no grace when it hits its time limit. kill_now and the drop behaviour are untouched; the new signal(sig) method is a separate addition.
• Wiring: quit calls the new stop_running in src/repl.rs after the main loop, so it covers every way out of the REPL. Every new definition has a caller in this diff.
• Tests: I re-ran only the background-job kill tests. All 10 passed in 2.45s, using real processes: a plain job ends with SIGTERM, a job that traps SIGTERM finishes its cleanup and keeps its own exit code (7), a job that ignores SIGTERM and its child both die by SIGKILL after the grace, a finished job keeps its code, and two jobs at quit share one grace.
• One existing #1077 test changed: its fixture now ignores SIGTERM, so it still checks the SIGKILL outcome. Its assertion is unchanged, and ARCHITECTURE.md explains why.
• Docs: docs/src/usage/commands.md and the ARCHITECTURE.md entries are updated.

One minor point, not a blocker: on non-Unix builds the new grace constant is never used, which could trigger a dead-code warning there. CI only builds on Linux.
